      "content": "#include <algorithm>\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nnamespace {\nusing u32 = std::uint32_t;\nusing u64 = std::uint64_t;\n\nconstexpr u64 kMod = 1000000007ULL;\nconstexpr int kBlock = 16;\n\n// c[j] = P_(n-j), so x^n = sum_j c[j] * x^j modulo Q.\nvoid square_reduce(std::vector<u32>& r,\n                   const std::vector<u32>& c,\n                   std::vector<u64>& work) {\n    const int n = static_cast<int>(r.size());\n    const int terms = 2 * n - 1;\n\n    // Symmetric convolution, with two contiguous input streams.\n    // Sixteen products plus a residue fit in an unsigned 64-bit sum:\n    // 16 * (kMod - 1)^2 + (kMod - 1) < 2^64.\n    for (int degree = 0; degree < terms; ++degree) {\n        int i = std::max(0, degree - n + 1);\n        const int stop = (degree + 1) / 2;\n        u64 pairs = 0;\n        while (i < stop) {\n            const int end = std::min(stop, i + kBlock);\n            for (; i < end; ++i) {\n                pairs += static_cast<u64>(r[i]) * r[degree - i];\n            }\n            pairs %= kMod;\n        }\n        u64 value = pairs + pairs;\n        if ((degree & 1) == 0) {\n            const u64 middle = r[degree / 2];\n            value += middle * middle;\n        }\n        work[degree] = value % kMod;\n    }\n\n    // Eliminate high degrees in descending order. Each block adds at\n    // most sixteen products to any coefficient before normalization.\n    for (int top = terms - 1; top >= n;) {\n        const int bottom = std::max(n, top - kBlock + 1);\n        for (int degree = top; degree >= bottom; --degree) {\n            const u32 factor = static_cast<u32>(work[degree] % kMod);\n            if (factor == 0) {\n                continue;\n            }\n            u64* window = work.data() + (degree - n);\n            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                window[j] += static_cast<u64>(factor) * c[j];\n            }\n        }\n        // Only this surviving window could have changed in the block.\n        for (int j = bottom - n; j < bottom; ++j) {\n            work[j] %= kMod;\n        }\n        top = bottom - 1;\n    }\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        r[i] = static_cast<u32>(work[i]);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid multiply_by_x(std::vector<u32>& r, const std::vector<u32>& c) {\n    const int n = static_cast<int>(r.size());\n    const u64 high = r[n - 1];\n    for (int i = n - 1; i > 0; --i) {\n        r[i] = static_cast<u32>((r[i - 1] + high * c[i]) % kMod);\n    }\n    r[0] = static_cast<u32>((high * c[0]) % kMod);\n}\n} // namespace\n\nint main() {\n    std::ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    std::cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    int n;\n    u64 h;\n    if (!(std::cin >> n >> h)) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    std::vector<u32> initial(n);\n    std::vector<u32> reduction(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        std::cin >> initial[i];\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        std::cin >> reduction[n - 1 - i];\n    }\n\n    if (h <= static_cast<u64>(n)) {\n        std::cout << initial[h - 1] << '\\n';\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    std::vector<u32> polynomial(n, 0);\n    std::vector<u64> work(2 * n - 1);\n\n    // Initialize the leading binary digit with x modulo Q.\n    if (n == 1) {\n        polynomial[0] = reduction[0];\n    } else {\n        polynomial[1] = 1;\n    }\n\n    const u64 exponent = h - 1;\n    u64 bit = 1;\n    while (bit <= exponent / 2) {\n        bit <<= 1;\n    }\n    for (bit >>= 1; bit != 0; bit >>= 1) {\n        square_reduce(polynomial, reduction, work);\n        if ((exponent & bit) != 0) {\n            multiply_by_x(polynomial, reduction);\n        }\n    }\n\n    u64 answer = 0;\n    for (int start = 0; start < n; start += kBlock) {\n        const int end = std::min(n, start + kBlock);\n        for (int i = start; i < end; ++i) {\n            answer += static_cast<u64>(polynomial[i]) * initial[i];\n        }\n        answer %= kMod;\n    }\n    std::cout << answer << '\\n';\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "path": "src/npbonacci.cpp"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Replacing dense matrix exponentiation with cache-resident polynomial arithmetic should reduce computation to O(n^2 log H), storage to O(n), and eliminate fixed-size matrix copies.",
  "notes": "Since x^n equals sum_j P_(n-j)*x^j modulo Q, mapping x^j to A_(j+1) makes the remainder of x^(H-1) produce the required recurrence term. The n=1 case uses x modulo Q = P1. Sixteen accumulated products plus a residue fit in uint64_t. No commands or candidate measurements were run; correctness and whole-program timing require controller evaluation.",
